/- ========================================================================= -/
/- Galois theory.                                                            -/
/-                                                                           -/
/- Galois groups and extensions, fixed fields, the theorem of Artin, the     -/
/- fundamental theorem of Galois theory, natural irrationalities, Galois     -/
/- groups of polynomials (discriminants, the alternating group criterion,    -/
/- cubics) and of finite fields, Hilbert 90, Kummer and Artin-Schreier       -/
/- extensions, radical extensions and radical elements, cyclotomic           -/
/- extensions, solvable towers, and solvability by radicals: in              -/
/- characteristic zero a polynomial is solvable by radicals if and only if   -/
/- its Galois group is solvable.                                             -/
/- ========================================================================= -/
/- File: HolLibrary/Galois.lean

  Lean4 translation by Chris Walton 2026

  A translation of HOL Light's `Library/galois.ml`, by hand, in file order,
  in the style of HolLibrary/FieldTheory.lean (see there and
  HolMath/Base.lean for the conventions).  galois.ml begins with
  `needs "Library/fieldtheory.ml"` and `needs "Library/symmetric_group.ml"`;
  the theorems it uses from them (and from the files they need) and from the
  core library are supplied via the CoreThms structures
  (SessionLibrary.Galois fills them).  Each section is a bundle (a
  `structure`) built by a `setup` function; SessionLibrary/Galois.lean
  builds the bundles in order and exports every name under its HOL Light
  name.

  EVALUATION ORDER.  OCaml evaluates function arguments, tuples and list
  literals right to left, so in a proof the rules nearer the end are
  evaluated first.  Where one proof applies two or more rules that print or
  use counters (MESON, SET_RULE, RING_RULE, ARITH_RULE, TAUT) they are bound
  first, in OCaml's order.
-/

